From: GTS9 Fedora port <gts9wifi@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/a6xx: only clear CM3_FAULT in a6xx_hfi_irq

a6xx_hfi_irq() reads REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO (which reports all
raw/unmasked status bits, including A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_MSGQ and
OOB ack bits) and writes the entire status value back to
REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_CLR.

On SM8550/Adreno 740, enable_irq(gmu->hfi_irq) in a6xx_gmu_resume() can
deliver a latched pending HFI interrupt on CPU0 right as the resuming
CPU runs a6xx_gmu_set_initial_freq() -> HFI_H2F_MSG_GX_BW_PERF_VOTE.
Clearing all bits in INTR_CLR wipes out A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_MSGQ
before a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt() polls it, causing a 1-second
timeout followed ~0.5 ms later by "Unexpected message id ... on the
response queue" on the next HFI vote.

Because REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_MASK only unmasks CM3_FAULT, restrict
the INTR_CLR write in a6xx_hfi_irq() to CM3_FAULT so it never clears
polled MSGQ or OOB ack bits.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -64,7 +64,8 @@ static irqreturn_t a6xx_hfi_irq(int irq, void *data)
 	u32 status;
 
 	status = gmu_read(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO);
-	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_CLR, status);
+	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_CLR,
+		  status & A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_CM3_FAULT);
 
 	if (status & A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_CM3_FAULT) {
 		dev_err_ratelimited(gmu->dev, "GMU firmware fault\n");
